  "live_records": {
    "q-126523": {
      "dom_id": "step-q126523",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Consider the arc of the curve $y=x-{x}^{4}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?",
      "worked_solution": "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=0$ to ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x-{x}^{4})=1-4{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{4})\\sqrt{1+{(1-4{x}^{3})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{4})\\sqrt{2-8{x}^{3}+16{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
