    "q-126580": {
      "dom_id": "step-q126580",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=0$ to ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi x\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\pi (1-0) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
